From c41a5cd1ebd0e23e5fc87670931e1624c6c2caf3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Season Tempo Build <claude@seasontempo.local>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 17:16:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Budget delete: show the confirmation, and resolve what blocks
 it

Clicking Delete on a budget line did nothing visible. No modal, no error, no
change to the row.

FinanceClient passed confirmingDelete, onCancelDelete, onConfirmDelete and
deleteError to BudgetTab. BudgetTab's parameter list did not name them, so they
were dropped there and never reached BudgetSection, whose own
`confirmingDelete = null` default then made the comparison against
`budget:<id>` permanently false.

The click handler fired and the state changed. Nothing rendered it. Nothing was
hidden, off-screen or behind a z-index, which is why the earlier
scroll-into-view change had no effect on it at all.

The confirmation is now a modal rather than an inline block. Inline is right in
a drawer, where it sits beside what it is about; inside an expandable table it
is unmounted the moment the coach collapses the category, stranding the state.
The modal renders once at section level, outside every group, and resolves the
row across all categories so a collapsed one still works.

BLOCKED IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION.

Telling a coach a transaction is in the way, without saying which one or where
to go, leaves them to close the box, open Transactions, and work out for
themselves what to change. Offering Delete while deletion is impossible invites
a click that can only fail.

So when something blocks it, the modal stops being a confirmation and becomes
the thing that resolves it: what is in the way -- description, date, amount --
and a way straight to it. Each blocker opens that transaction, where its budget
line can be changed. The list caps at five so a line with forty transactions
does not produce a modal taller than the screen. There is no Delete button.

Nothing is moved automatically. Reassigning is a decision about financial
history and stays with the coach, and the server still refuses without an
explicit destination.

Convention check 4j is added for the original fault: a prop a parent passes must
be destructured by the child. Like 4i, this is a default value turning a wiring
mistake into a silently wrong screen instead of an error.
